      "text": "To find the greatest value of \\( x \\) that is a positive multiple of \\( 3 \\) and such that \\( x^3 < 1000 \\), we need to solve the inequality:\n\n\\[ x^3 < 1000. \\]\n\nFirst, we find the cube root of \\( 1000 \\):\n\n\\[ \\sqrt[3]{1000} = 10. \\]\n\nSo, \\( x^3 < 1000 \\) implies:\n\n\\[ x < 10. \\]\n\nSince \\( x \\) is a positive multiple of \\( 3 \\), the possible values for \\( x \\) are \\( 3, 6, \\) and \\( 9 \\). We need to check which of these values satisfies the inequality \\( x^3 < 1000 \\):\n\n- For \\( x = 3 \\):\n\n\\[ 3^3 = 27, \\]\n\nwhich is less than \\( 1000 \\).\n\n- For \\( x = 6 \\):\n\n\\[ 6^3 = 216, \\]\n\nwhich is also less than \\( 1000 \\).\n\n- For \\( x = 9 \\):\n\n\\[ 9^3 = 729, \\]\n\nwhich is less than \\( 1000 \\).\n\n- For \\( x = 10 \\):\n\n\\[ 10^3 = 1000, \\]\n\nwhich is not less than \\( 1000 \\).\n\nTherefore, the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) is \\( 9 \\). The final answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{9} \\]",
