      "text": "We are tasked with finding the greatest positive multiple of \\( 3 \\) such that \\( x^3 < 1000 \\). This means \\( x < \\sqrt[3]{1000} \\). Since \\( 1000 = 10^3 \\), \\( \\sqrt[3]{1000} = 10 \\). Therefore, the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) should be \\( 9 \\) because \\( 9 \\) is the only multiple of \\( 3 \\) less than \\( 10 \\) and \\( 9^3 = 729 \\) which is less than \\( 1000 \\).\n\nLet's verify this using code.\n```python\n# Define the upper limit as the cube root of 1000\nupper_limit = 1000 ** (1/3)\n\n# Find the greatest integer less than the upper limit\ngreatest_multiple = int(upper_limit)\n\n# Ensure we are dealing with a multiple of 3\nwhile greatest_multiple % 3 != 0:\n    greatest_multiple -= 1\n\n# Verify if the greatest_multiple cubed is less than 1000\nif greatest_multiple ** 3 < 1000:\n    greatest_multiple = greatest_multiple\nelse:\n    greatest_multiple = greatest_multiple - 1\n\nprint(greatest_multiple)\n```\n```output\n9\n```\nThe greatest possible value of \\( x \\) that is a positive multiple of \\( 3 \\) and whose cube is less than \\( 1000 \\) is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).",
